now the world health organization has  declared the fast-spreading monkey pox  outbreak a global health emergency it's  the who's highest level of alert it was  issued by the un health agency chief  despite a deadlock among experts over  the decision  thinks the risk of the virus is high in  europe and moderate globally more than  70 nations are now experiencing an  outbreak of the virus which is common in  parts of central and west africa  we have an outbreak that has spread  around the world rapidly through new  models of transmission about which we  understand too little  and which meets the criteria in the  international health regulations  for all of these reasons  i have decided that the global monkey  box outbreak represents a public health  emergency of international concern  lawrence uh gosten is the director of  the wh so center on global health law  he's also the founding o'neill chair in  the global health law at georgetown  university and he joins us live from  washington dc thanks very much indeed  for being on the program so just how  worried should we be about monkey pox  now  well i mean we should absolutely be  worried um  we've seen an exponential rise in monkey  pox cases it's now  in all in five wh  regions of the world  in  many many dozens of countries  with cases rising exponentially  so it's of concern right now  um outside of uh endemic areas in in  central and east africa  it's primarily but not exclusively but  primarily  within  communities of men who have sex with men  but that should not lead to complacency  that that community  matters a lot  and it also could spill over easily to  other communities as we've seen with the  hiv epidemic and give us an idea then  what are the symptoms what should people  be watching out for if they're watching  this now  well you know there there are  characteristic monkey pox um rashes um  that they should be looking at  um particularly if they're in a an  elevated risk group that if they've had  um uh intimate sexual relationships or  close physical contact with somebody who  might be ill  there should be contact tracing  there should be widespread testing and  there should be  a  strategic deployment of  vaccines to really try to nip this in  the bud but the window for containment  of monkey pox is  rapidly closing and you know we we fear  um that this could become endemic  in europe north america and other parts  of the world  over the next months and years given  what you say then why do you think it's  appearing in regions where it hasn't  done so  before  well that's exactly what dr tedros the  director general is is pointing out  we we've never before had  um such widespread outbreaks of cases  that are not travel related  to africa  clearly there are  new or different modes of transmission  clearly it's gotten a foothold  particularly in europe and north america  but there's no reason why it should not  spread more widely  outside of those regions  just as we've seen with other sexually  transmitted infections like  hiv we've really got to take this  seriously and i do applaud  um dr tedros for declaring an emergency  it was politically brave in fact  it's the first time in history a  director general has declared an  emergency without a clear recommendation  from the emergency committee of the  world health organization so he's done a  politically courageous thing and we all  need to thank him because it's better to  err  on the side of action than in action  well in fact we we understand that he  was something of a tiebreaker on the who  committee to make this declaration so it  was a close call then  it was  you know you remember it was only a few  weeks ago  that dr tejo said  convene the emergency committee and they  decided  you know by a significant majority not  to declare an emergency i always thought  that was wrong i thought that they  should have  the international health regulations  criteria were clearly met  um when tajos  re-convened the committee i think he was  sending  a signal to the committee that he was  really worried  this time it was much closer although  the majority of the committee a slight  majority  voted against the emergency  but tejos  decided and he was legally entitled to  do that  but this was an emergency and declared  it such and i'm really um delighted to  see the kind of courageous leadership  he's shown here  okay we'll leave it there lawrence  augustine we very much appreciate your  time here on al jazeera thank you
 

